{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/scarred-lands/1022795-ask-a-simple-question-scarred-lands","id":1022795,"title":"Ask a simple question, Scarred Lands","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scarred Lands"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-12-27T09:20:54-08:00","id":1022795,"title":"Ask a simple question, Scarred Lands","content":"I had a quick, simple question that didn't seem worth starting it's own thread for, so I figured I should make one of these for Scarred Lands. <br /> <br /> So, as for my question, which regards the 5e version. I noticed that Aasatthi and Slitherin both have traits that allow them to perform a special movement type without expending extra Movement (swimming and climbing, respectively). I was wondering if these features were worded this way intentionally, rather than giving them swim/climb speeds, to avoid interactions with other rules that check whether a creature has a special Movement type speed. For example, the underwater combat rules affect creatures with swim speeds differently than creatures without swim speeds. Is it intentional that Assatthi are affected as though they don't have a swim speed, despite the fact that they can swim at the same speed as a creature that has a 30 foot swim speed?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T17:31:54-08:00","id":1023501,"title":"","content":"Hey Charlaquin. Yes, that's right -- the wording is intended to interact with the rules in a certain way. Both asaatthi and slitherin are able to move more quickly than other humanoids while swimming or climbing, but they're still not as adept at those modes of movement as a creature native to that element or environment, such as a spider or a fish, respectively.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12432-scott-holden","name":"Scott Holden","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12432_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T18:39:42-08:00","id":1023531,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Scott Holden</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/scarred-lands/1022795-ask-a-simple-question-scarred-lands?p=1023501#post1023501\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hey Charlaquin. Yes, that's right -- the wording is intended to interact with the rules in a certain way. Both asaatthi and slitherin are able to move more quickly than other humanoids while swimming or climbing, but they're still not as adept at those modes of movement as a creature native to that element or environment, such as a spider or a fish, respectively.</div> </div> </div> </div>Awesome, thank you!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T22:19:18-08:00","id":1023593,"title":"","content":"Another quick question about the 4e version - is there a reason other than flavor that tail blades are light weapons? The special property of tail blades allows you to attack with one as a bonus action when you take the Attack action, which would seem to make the Light property pointless, as all it does is allow you to attack with it as a bonus action when you make an attack with another Light weapon.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-24T03:10:50-07:00","id":1210878,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/scarred-lands/1022795-ask-a-simple-question-scarred-lands?p=1023593#post1023593\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another quick question about the 4e version - is there a reason other than flavor that tail blades are light weapons? The special property of tail blades allows you to attack with one as a bonus action when you take the Attack action, which would seem to make the Light property pointless, as all it does is allow you to attack with it as a bonus action when you make an attack with another Light weapon.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Looks like I missed this post a long while back. Apologies. <br /> <br /> The &quot;light&quot; distinction is really for the purpose of future-proofing: If a feat or class ability or something comes along that interacts only with light weapons, or there is a magic weapon that can only be a light weapon, for example, then the tail blade would still be eligible. But you are entirely correct that its special qualities otherwise make the fact that it is light redundant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12432-scott-holden","name":"Scott Holden","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12432_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}